  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Documenting Ancient Sagalassos shows clear early use: it has 2,366 recorded downloads and has already been cited once, indicating that the book is reaching readers and beginning to enter scholarly discussion. The record also shows institutional support from KU Leuven and open availability through a hybrid-access route, which likely helps discoverability and reuse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Documenting Ancient Sagalassos is an archaeology book published by Leuven University Press in 2023, authored by Jeroen Poblome. Its impact record is still early, but it is already more than merely visible: the book has accumulated 2,366 downloads, suggesting substantial reader interest and active consultation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is present but modest so far. OpenAlex records one citation, appearing in 2024 in Antiquity through the article \u201cNew Book Chronicle.\u201d That single citation is best read as an initial sign that the book has entered disciplinary conversation, rather than as evidence of broad citation reach at this stage.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The content profile aligns closely with its field position. The strongest concepts attached to the work center on curiosity, archaeology, and natural archaeology, with history also present. This fits a book that appears to speak both to specialist archaeological practice and to the broader intellectual appeal of documenting a major ancient site.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are supportive rather than headline-making. The book is listed as open access in the available repository record, and WorldCat holdings indicate library discoverability. Funding metadata names KU Leuven, which provides additional context for the work\u2019s institutional support. Public event data were not available in the record, so the narrative rests on the evidence that is actually captured: usage, one early citation, and clear signs of academic visibility.</p>",
